```python
def calculate_derivative():
    """
    Calculate the derivative of a function using the Average Rate of Change Formula.
    """
    # Get user input for the function and its derivative values
    func_name = input("Enter the name of the function: ")
    x = float(input("Enter the value of x: "))
    f_x = float(input(f"Enter the value of f(x) at x = {x}: "))
    f_x_derivative = float(input(f"Enter the value of f'(x) at x = {x}: "))

    # Calculate the derivative using the Average Rate of Change Formula
    derivative = (f_x - f_x_derivative) / (x - x)

    # Print the result
    print(f"The derivative of {func_name} at x = {x} is {derivative}")

# Run the script
calculate_derivative()
```

To use this script, simply run it and follow the prompts to input the function and its derivative values. The script will then calculate and print the derivative of the input function.

Example use case:

```
Enter the name of the function: f(x)
Enter the value of x: 2
Enter the value of f(x) at x = 2: 10
Enter the value of f'(x) at x = 2: 5
The derivative of f(x) at x = 2 is 5.0
```

This script assumes that the input function is a polynomial of degree 1 (i.e., a linear function). If the input function is not a polynomial, the script will not produce accurate results.

